# ---- GOLDEN FIXTURE (bridge tile) ----
# resetMode=loadvm -> restore the INTERNAL 'golden' snapshot: X (-nocursor) +
# one fullscreen xterm parked on the PDP-11 chooser, blocked in `read`, with no
# simulator running. Reset therefore does two things at once: it returns the
# visitor to a screen that names all three operating systems, and it throws away
# whatever they did inside one of them (each system runs on a sparse scratch
# copy of its pack under /tmp, so the master packs are never written either).
SH_RESET_MODE=loadvm
SH_GOLDEN_SNAPSHOT=golden
SH_FIXTURE_DESC=A DEC PDP-11 with three of its operating systems behind a chooser. The golden is the chooser itself: a green-on-black 80x31 xterm on a 1024x768 X root listing "1 RT-11 V5.3 (single-user real-time monitor, 11/73, RL02)", "2 RSX-11M V4.2 (multi-user real-time executive, 11/70, RD52)" and "3 RSTS/E V9.6 (multi-user timesharing, 11/70, RD54)", each with the prompt it answers with (. > $) spelled out beside it, over the line "Press 1, 2 or 3. Reset returns you to this screen." The RSTS/E entry carries three extra lines saying plainly that its installer stops one step short, because the second "Library" tape it asks for no longer exists: DCL answers but the packaged commands were never wired up. One keypress boots the chosen system under Open SIMH (pinned to commit a1f57fa3, built into this tile's overlay because the frozen bridge base predates it). RSX-11M is V4.2 BL38, not the V4.3 ceiling of the Mentec grant, because no reachable copy of V4.3 exists; the chooser says V4.2 rather than pretending. Keyboard-only exhibit; no pointer device; the AC97 card is present because the bridge shape always carries it, but nothing inside the guest makes a sound.

# ---- BINARY: the vic20/plus4 CANARY, not the shared fleet build ----
# /usr/local/lib/streamhost/tiles/decos/current points at
# streamhost-bca88a2bed22e1ea616993995faf4379b954bb11, the same build vic20 and
# plus4 run. Not for key pacing (see below) but for SH_INPUT_BACKEND=disabled:
# the promoted fleet binary (d2652847) panics on it —
#   invalid SH_INPUT_BACKEND="disabled"; expected dbus-abs|dbus-rel|warpd|...
# and this tile's QEMU device set carries no pointing device at all, so naming
# a dbus pointer backend the way c64 does would be a fiction.

# ---- KEYBOARD PACING (the exhibit's only input surface) ----
# NO SH_KEY_MIN_HOLD_MS / SH_KEY_MIN_GAP_MS, deliberately.
#
# The frame-sampling trap of playbook 5.1 is an EMULATOR-FRAME problem: MAME and
# VICE sample the key matrix once per emulated frame, so the release-to-press gap
# has to survive a frame period. SIMH has no such loop -- its console is a byte
# stream -- and that was measured, not assumed: a 69-character line written to
# the console at a 0 ms inter-character gap echoed and executed intact, 5 trials
# out of 5. What is left is the ordinary QEMU PS/2 -> X -> xterm path that
# alpine, tinycore and haiku already run unpaced, and the exhibit's own input
# requirement is one digit. The builder presses that digit through QMP
# input-send-event with a 80 ms hold and no gap, and asserts by framebuffer that
# the chooser is gone and by pgrep that a simulator is running.
